    {
      "column_index": 2,
      "header_id": "C321",
      "x_range": [
        1163.7,
        2261.3
      ],
      "section_title": "Brep: What is it?",
      "section_number": 2,
      "teaching_text": "2- Brep: What is it?\r\n\r\n\"The word Brep stands for Boundary Representation. That in itself doesn't tell you much, but allow me to break down the general idea behind Breps. In Rhino UI itself, we don't use the word Brep, that one is only for programmers. Instead \"Polysurface\" is used, but it is not an ideal replacement because a Brep may well have only a single surface.\"\r\n- David Rutten\r\n\r\nSource:\r\n\r\nhttp://www.grasshopper3d.com/forum/topics/how-to-make-a-brep\r\n\r\n- In Grasshopper, a Brep is a NURBS goemetry, and not a Mesh.\r\n\r\nWhat is modeled in Rhino is a Surface or a Polysurface or a Mesh.\r\n\r\nWhen a Polysurface is being referenced in Grasshopper, it would be called as a Brep and not a polysurface.\r\n\r\n- The \"Mesh Brep\" component converts a Brep into a Mesh.\r\n\r\n\"NOTE: it is generally much easier to convert from a NURBS geometry to a mesh object rather than the other way around. While the UV coordinates of a NURBS surface are straightforward to convert to quad faces of a mesh, the opposite is not necessarily true, since a mesh might contain a combination of triangles and quads in a way that is not simple to extract a UV coordinate system out of.\"\r\n\r\nSource:\r\nhttp://grasshopperprimer.com/en/1-foundations/1-6/3_Creating%20Meshes.html",
      "teaching_panel_id": "C320",
      "additional_teaching_panels": [
        {
          "id": "C163",
          "text": "3- Brep Analysis:\r\n\r\nLike the \"Evaluate Surface\" component, Breps also have similar components that let us extract information and manipulate them.\r\n\r\n- Make a simple \"Center Box\" component.\r\n\r\n- Use the \"Deconstruct Brep\" component to deconstruct the Box into its basic geometries:\r\n1- Faces (surfaces)\r\n2- Edges\r\n3- Vertecies\r\n\r\n- Use the \"Brep Edges\" component to only extract edges.\r\nNote that this component provides few more options relating to the nature of the edges.\r\nHover the cursor over the OUTPUTS and check them out.\r\n\r\n- Use the \"Brep Wireframe\" component to only extract edges. This is similar to the \"Deconstruct Brep\"'s Edges OUTPUT.\r\n\r\n- Check out the \"Shape In Brep\" and see how it can be useful to you."
        }
      ],
